    "content": "High blood pressure (hypertension) is often called the \"silent killer\" because it typically causes no symptoms until it causes significant damage. When symptoms do occur—usually at very high levels (hypertensive crisis)—they may include: Severe Headaches: Often described as pulsating, throbbing, or occurring in the morning. Shortness of Breath: Difficulty breathing, even with mild exertion. Blurred or Double Vision: Visual changes or eye strain. Chest Pain or Pressure: Tightness in the chest. Dizziness or Lightheadedness: Feeling unsteady or fainting. Other potential, less common signs include nosebleeds, facial flushing, and heart palpitations. Because symptoms are rare until serious complications arise, regular blood pressure screenings are essential. When to Seek Immediate Medical HelpIf you experience severe headaches, severe chest pain, or vision changes combined with confusion or weakness, seek emergency medical care, as these could indicate a hypertensive crisis.",
